DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The wwccssttookk() function is the wide‐character equivalent of the ssttrrttookk(3)
       function,  with  an added argument to make it multithread‐safe.  It can
       be used to split a wide‐character string _w_c_s into tokens, where a token
       is defined as a substring not containing any wide‐characters  from  _d_e_‐
       _l_i_m.

       The  search  starts  at  _w_c_s, if _w_c_s is not NULL, or at _*_p_t_r, if _w_c_s is
       NULL.  First, any delimiter wide‐characters are skipped, that  is,  the
       pointer  is  advanced  beyond any wide‐characters which occur in _d_e_l_i_m.
       If the end of the wide‐character string is now  reached,  wwccssttookk()  re‐
       turns NULL, to indicate that no tokens were found, and stores an appro‐
       priate  value  in  _*_p_t_r, so that subsequent calls to wwccssttookk() will con‐
       tinue to return NULL.  Otherwise, the wwccssttookk() function recognizes  the
       beginning  of  a  token  and  returns a pointer to it, but before doing
       that, it zero‐terminates the token by replacing the next wide‐character
       which occurs in _d_e_l_i_m with a null wide character (L'\0'),  and  it  up‐
       dates  _*_p_t_r  so that subsequent calls will continue searching after the
       end of recognized token.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The wwccssttookk() function returns a pointer to the next token, or  NULL  if
       no further token was found.

